# The kernel: the forms wap's emitter understands, and nothing else (notes/kernel.md).
#
# Each form is a pattern type of Node: one declaration builds the form (`Call(f, args)`), tests it (`n is Call`),
# takes it apart (`match n { Call(f, args) => … }`) and reads it (`n.callee` of a parameter `n: Call`). Everything a
# program writes is rewritten into these forms before the emitter sees it (wap/core/rules/lowering.wap).
#
# Milestone 1 (`print "Hello"`) needs two forms; the others of notes/kernel.md join as programs need them. The heads
# are plain words: only the lowering builds kernel nodes, so a program's own `(call …)` group never reaches the
# emitter as one. The reserved `·call` comes when the reader reads it as one word (notes/kernel.md, open point 1).
# Literals (texts, numbers) are the Node cases themselves and need no declaration.

# a call of a function with its arguments, a [list] of nodes: `print "Hello"` is Call(print, ["Hello"])
type Call < Node = (call callee: Node arguments: Node)

# statements run in order, a [list] of nodes: a program, a block
type Seq < Node = (seq statements: Node)
